**Why tests miss it:** Tests search for exact case matches. Searching for " Alexander " (with spaces) or "alexander" (lowercase) might fail or return different results.  
**Recommended test fix:**
```csharp
[Fact]
public void Index_SearchWithLeadingTrailingSpaces_StillMatches()
{
    var result = _controller.Index(null, null, "  Alexander  ", null);
    var model = ((ViewResult)result).Model as PaginatedList<Student>;
    Assert.Single(model);
}

[Fact]
public void Index_SearchCaseInsensitive_FindsMatch()
{
    // If the code doesn't normalize case, this will fail
    var result = _controller.Index(null, null, "ALEXANDER", null);
    var model = ((ViewResult)result).Model as PaginatedList<Student>;
    // This test will fail if .ToLower() is not applied
}
```

---

### **4. BaseController.cs**

#### **GAP 1: userName hardcoded to "System" in SendEntityNotification**
**Location:** `/tmp/sv-a20148d65bd94ec09ec88f84df0a53cc/ContosoUniversity/Controllers/BaseController.cs`, Line 29  
**Original:** `var userName = "System"; // No authentication, use System as default user`  
**Mutation:** `var userName = null;` or `var userName = "";`  
**Why tests miss it:** Test `SendEntityNotification_ThreeArgs_EnqueuesNotification` (line 50) verifies CreatedBy is "System", but doesn't verify this is because the controller hardcodes it, not because NotificationService defaults it.  
**Recommended test fix:**
```csharp
[Fact]
public void SendEntityNotification_AlwaysSendsAsSystemUser()
{
    // Send with explicit null in notification service (to bypass its default)
    // Then verify controller override still makes it "System"
    _controller.TestSendEntityNotification("Entity", "1", EntityOperation.CREATE);

    var svc = new NotificationService();
    var notification = svc.ReceiveNotification();
    svc.Dispose();

    // Must be "System" because controller hardcodes it
    Assert.Equal("System", notification!.CreatedBy);
}
```

#### **GAP 2: Exception swallowed in SendEntityNotification**
**Location:** `/tmp/sv-a20148d65bd94ec09ec88f84df0a53cc/ContosoUniversity/Controllers/BaseController.cs`, Lines 27-36  
**Original:**
```csharp
try
{
    var userName = "System";
    notificationService.SendNotification(entityType, entityId, entityDisplayName, operation, userName);
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    System.Diagnostics.Debug.WriteLine($"Failed to send notification: {ex.Message}");
}
```
**Mutation:** `throw;` instead of Debug.WriteLine (or re-throw exception)  
**Why tests miss it:** No test validates that exceptions don't propagate. If SendNotification throws, controller action should still complete.  
**Recommended test fix:**
```csharp
[Fact]
public void SendEntityNotification_WithBadData_DoesNotThrow()
{
    // Even with malformed data, should not throw
    var ex = Record.Exception(() =>
        _controller.TestSendEntityNotification(null, null, null, EntityOperation.CREATE));

    Assert.Null(ex);
}
```

#### **GAP 3: Dispose doesn't verify null coalescing**
**Location:** `/tmp/sv-a20148d65bd94ec09ec88f84df0a53cc/ContosoUniversity/Controllers/BaseController.cs`, Lines 39-46  
**Original:**
```csharp
protected override void Dispose(bool disposing)
{
    if (disposing)
    {
        db?.Dispose();
        notificationService?.Dispose();
    }
    base.Dispose(disposing);
}
```
**Mutation:** Remove null coalescing operators (`db?.Dispose()` → `db.Dispose()`)  
**Why tests miss it:** Test only calls Dispose once (line 94-102). Doesn't test the case where db or notificationService might be null.  
**Recommended test fix:**
```csharp
[Fact]
public void Dispose_WithNullDb_DoesNotThrow()
{
    // This is hard to test since constructor sets db immediately
    // But test should verify null-safety is preserved
    var ex = Record.Exception(() =>
    {
        _controller.Dispose();
        _controller.Dispose(); // Call twice to test idempotency
    });

    Assert.Null(ex);
}
```

